<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Arkwright</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1732">1732</a>–<a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard Arkwright was an English inventor and industrialist, often called the father of the factory system. He patented the water frame, a spinning machine driven by water power, in 1769, and from 1771 built a water-powered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mill at Cromford in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>, with housing for its workers. His mills were widely copied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and abroad, and he was knighted in 1786.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-arkwright.jpg" alt="Richard Arkwright" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mather Brown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Richard_Arkwright_by_Mather_Brown_1790.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
